  completeExplanation: 'The chapter opens with an inquiry-based magic message demonstration on National Science Day, revealing how colorless chemical reagents produce vivid color shifts upon contact. Substances are systematically classified by their chemical behavior into three distinct categories: acidic, basic, and neutral. Acids, characterized by their sharp, sour taste, turn blue litmus paper red. Natural edible acids include citric acid (citrus fruits), ascorbic acid (amla/Vitamin C), tartaric acid (tamarind/unripe mango), lactic acid (curd), acetic acid (vinegar), and oxalic acid (tomatoes). Bases, characterized by a bitter taste and slippery/soapy feel, turn red litmus blue; common examples include soap solution, baking soda, washing powder, and lime water (calcium hydroxide prepared from calcium oxide/chuna). Substances showing no change on either litmus are neutral (e.g., pure water, salt solution, sugar solution). The chapter explores rich botanical indicators: Litmus (naturally extracted from lichens, which represent a clean-air symbiotic mutualism between fungi and algae), Red Rose Petal Extract (which turns red/magenta in acids and green in bases), Turmeric/Haldi (which turns deep red exclusively in basic solutions, explaining why soap turns yellow curry stains red and enabling greeting-card art), and Olfactory Indicators (onion strips or vanilla whose pungent odours vanish in basic medium, offering inclusive sensory testing for visually challenged learners). Highlighting Indian scientific heritage, the chapter honors Acharya Prafulla Chandra Ray ("Father of Modern Indian Chemistry" and founder of Bengal Chemicals). The core chemical concept is the Neutralisation Reaction: when an acid and a base mix in stoichiometric proportions, their properties cancel each other out to form a salt, water, and liberate heat (an exothermic process). Daily life applications include neutralizing formic acid injected by red ant bites using moist baking soda (sodium hydrogen carbonate) or calamine lotion (zinc carbonate), treating fertilizer-damaged acidic soils with quicklime (calcium oxide) or slaked lime (calcium hydroxide), treating alkaline soils with acid-releasing organic compost, and treating toxic acidic industrial effluents before discharge into rivers to protect aquatic life. The chapter concludes with acid-carbonate effervescence, showing that vinegar reacts with calcium carbonate in eggshells to liberate bubbling carbon dioxide gas.',
